{"title":"01 \u2014 Data Engineer Track","relativePath":"Engineering-KB/Learning-Paths/01_DATA_ENGINEER_TRACK.md","html":"\u003Ch1\u003E01 \u2014 Data Engineer Track\u003C/h1\u003E\n\u003Cblockquote\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe one idea:\u003C/strong\u003E a data engineer turns raw, messy, late data into tables people can trust, and keeps doing it every day without surprises. Learn the vocabulary, then SQL and Python, then one platform deeply, then reliability, then design.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/blockquote\u003E\n\u003Chr /\u003E\n\u003Ch2\u003E1. Level 1: Foundation (4 to 6 weeks)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGoal:\u003C/strong\u003E speak the language, query data, and run a notebook.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ctable\u003E\n\u003Cthead\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Cth\u003ERead, in order\u003C/th\u003E\n\u003Cth\u003EWhy\u003C/th\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003C/thead\u003E\n\u003Ctbody\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Data-Engineering/01_DATA_ENGINEERING_FOUNDATIONS.md\u0022\u003EData Engineering Foundations\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003ERoles, lifecycle, OLTP vs OLAP\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Data-Engineering/02_DATA_ENGINEERING_GLOSSARY.md\u0022\u003EData Engineering Glossary\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/14_DATA_TERMINOLOGY_A_TO_Z.md\u0022\u003EData Terminology A to Z\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EVocabulary. Keep both open\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Topics/11_SQL.md\u0022\u003ESQL\u003C/a\u003E, then \u003Ca href=\u0022../Financial-Data-Engine/02_FOUNDATIONS.md\u0022\u003EFoundations: windows, CTEs, MERGE\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003ESQL is most of the job\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../18_PYTHON_AND_DATA_ENGINEERING.md\u0022\u003EPython \u0026amp; Data Engineering\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EPython for data\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Topics/16_GIT.md\u0022\u003EGit\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EEverything lives in Git\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Data-Engineering/12_DATABRICKS_HANDS_ON_FROM_ZERO.md\u0022\u003EDatabricks Hands-On from Zero\u003C/a\u003E, labs 1\u20136\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EFirst real pipeline\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003C/tbody\u003E\n\u003C/table\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject:\u003C/strong\u003E the Bronze, Silver and Gold pipeline from the hands-on labs, with a README explaining each layer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChecklist:\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul class=\u0022contains-task-list\u0022\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E I can explain Bronze, Silver and Gold, and OLTP vs OLAP, in my own words.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E I can write joins, aggregates, window functions and a CTE without looking anything up.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E I can read a CSV into a table, type the columns, and deduplicate by key.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E My project is in Git with a README.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Chr /\u003E\n\u003Ch2\u003E2. Level 2: Practitioner (2 to 3 months)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGoal:\u003C/strong\u003E build pipelines that are incremental, tested and repeatable.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ctable\u003E\n\u003Cthead\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Cth\u003ERead, in order\u003C/th\u003E\n\u003Cth\u003EWhy\u003C/th\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003C/thead\u003E\n\u003Ctbody\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Data-Engineering/03_DATA_PROCESSING_TYPES.md\u0022\u003EData Processing Types\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EBatch, streaming, CDC, load strategies\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Data-Engineering/04_STORAGE_FORMATS_AND_LAYOUT.md\u0022\u003EStorage Formats and Layout\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/04_PARQUET_ARROW_AND_THE_COLUMNAR_STACK.md\u0022\u003EParquet and Arrow\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EFiles and layout\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Data-Engineering/05_DATA_MODELING.md\u0022\u003EData Modeling\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EFacts, dimensions, SCD\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Data-Engineering/06_APACHE_SPARK.md\u0022\u003EApache Spark\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\u0022../Data-Engineering/13_PYSPARK_DATAFRAME_COOKBOOK.md\u0022\u003EPySpark Cookbook\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EThe engine and its API\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Data-Engineering/08_DELTA_LAKE.md\u0022\u003EDelta Lake\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/01_ICEBERG_TABLE_ANATOMY.md\u0022\u003EIceberg Table Anatomy\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003ETable formats\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../26_POLARS_DEEP_DIVE.md\u0022\u003EPolars\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/11_SINGLE_NODE_ENGINES.md\u0022\u003ESingle-Node Engines\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EWhen you don\u0027t need a cluster\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Data-Engineering/11_PIPELINE_PATTERNS_AND_PRACTICES.md\u0022\u003EPipeline Patterns and Practices\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EIdempotency, backfills, testing\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/06_LAKEFLOW_CONNECT_PIPELINES_AND_JOBS.md\u0022\u003ELakeflow\u003C/a\u003E, hands-on labs 7\u20138\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EDeclarative pipelines and jobs\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003C/tbody\u003E\n\u003C/table\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject:\u003C/strong\u003E an incremental pipeline from a file drop and a simulated CDC feed, into an SCD2 dimension and a fact table, with expectations, quarantine, unit tests and a scheduled job.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChecklist:\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul class=\u0022contains-task-list\u0022\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E Re-running my pipeline on the same input changes nothing (idempotent).\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E I can backfill one month without double counting.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E Late and out-of-order events land in the right place.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E I can read the Spark UI and point to the stage that shuffles.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E My pipeline has tests that run in CI.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Chr /\u003E\n\u003Ch2\u003E3. Level 3: Professional (6 to 12 months of practice)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGoal:\u003C/strong\u003E reliability, governance, streaming and cost.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ctable\u003E\n\u003Cthead\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Cth\u003ERead, in order\u003C/th\u003E\n\u003Cth\u003EWhy\u003C/th\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003C/thead\u003E\n\u003Ctbody\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Data-Engineering/09_STREAMING_PIPELINES.md\u0022\u003EStreaming Pipelines\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EState, watermarks, exactly-once\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Data-Engineering/10_ORCHESTRATION_QUALITY_AND_GOVERNANCE.md\u0022\u003EOrchestration, Quality and Governance\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EContracts, observability, access\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/13_CONTRACTS_LINEAGE_AND_OBSERVABILITY.md\u0022\u003EContracts, Lineage and Observability\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/16_STATISTICS_FOR_DATA_QUALITY_AND_DRIFT.md\u0022\u003EDrift Statistics\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EProve that data is right\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/02_ICEBERG_DELETES_V3_AND_MAINTENANCE.md\u0022\u003EIceberg Deletes, v3 and Maintenance\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/03_ICEBERG_CATALOGS_AND_INTEROPERABILITY.md\u0022\u003ECatalogs\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003ERunning open tables at scale\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/05_UNITY_CATALOG_IN_DEPTH.md\u0022\u003EUnity Catalog in Depth\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EGovernance\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/07_DATABRICKS_SQL_COMPUTE_AND_PERFORMANCE.md\u0022\u003EDatabricks SQL, Compute and Performance\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003ESpeed and cost\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/17_PERSONAL_DATA_AND_INDIAS_DPDP_ACT.md\u0022\u003EPersonal Data and DPDP\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EPrivacy duties\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003Ctr\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ca href=\u0022../Financial-Data-Engine/00_INDEX.md\u0022\u003EFinancial Data Engine course, modules 00\u201312\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003Ctd\u003EOne full domain engine, end to end\u003C/td\u003E\n\u003C/tr\u003E\n\u003C/tbody\u003E\n\u003C/table\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject:\u003C/strong\u003E the \u003Ca href=\u0022../Financial-Data-Engine/12_CAPSTONE_PROJECT.md\u0022\u003EFinancial Data Engine capstone\u003C/a\u003E, extended with write-audit-publish, an ODCS contract checked in CI, column lineage, and a freshness monitor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChecklist:\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul class=\u0022contains-task-list\u0022\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E A failed batch is invisible to consumers (write-audit-publish).\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E Every table has an owner, a contract and a freshness target.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E I can explain the cost of each job and cut it by a measured amount.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E I can trace a wrong number to its source file and the job that produced it.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E Bitemporal \u0026quot;as of\u0026quot; queries return the right answer for a past date.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Chr /\u003E\n\u003Ch2\u003E4. Level 4: Senior\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGoal:\u003C/strong\u003E design platforms, set standards, and lead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDesign:\u003C/strong\u003E choose formats, catalogs and engines for a whole platform, and defend the trade-offs (\u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/03_ICEBERG_CATALOGS_AND_INTEROPERABILITY.md\u0022\u003ECatalogs and Interoperability\u003C/a\u003E, \u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/15_WHATS_NEW_IN_THE_LAKEHOUSE_2026.md\u0022\u003EWhat\u0027s New 2026\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStandards:\u003C/strong\u003E contracts, naming, review checklists, testing rules, runbooks.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOperations:\u003C/strong\u003E lead incidents, write blameless post-incident reviews, set SLOs (\u003Ca href=\u0022../23_OBSERVABILITY_AND_PERFORMANCE.md\u0022\u003EObservability\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPeople:\u003C/strong\u003E review designs and code, mentor L1 to L3 engineers.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStay current:\u003C/strong\u003E follow the release feeds (\u003Ca href=\u0022../Open-Lakehouse/19_STAYING_CURRENT_NEWS_SOURCES_AND_FEEDS.md\u0022\u003Enews sources\u003C/a\u003E) and the What\u0027s New notes.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChecklist:\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul class=\u0022contains-task-list\u0022\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E I have written a design document that others built from.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E I have led an incident from detection to a published review.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E I can explain why our platform uses each format, catalog and engine, and when that should change.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli class=\u0022task-list-item\u0022\u003E\u003Cinput disabled=\u0022disabled\u0022 type=\u0022checkbox\u0022 /\u003E Engineers I mentored have moved up a level.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003C/ul\u003E\n","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"01 \u2014 Data Engineer Track","anchor":"01-data-engineer-track"},{"level":2,"text":"1. Level 1: Foundation (4 to 6 weeks)","anchor":"1-level-1-foundation-4-to-6-weeks"},{"level":2,"text":"2. Level 2: Practitioner (2 to 3 months)","anchor":"2-level-2-practitioner-2-to-3-months"},{"level":2,"text":"3. Level 3: Professional (6 to 12 months of practice)","anchor":"3-level-3-professional-6-to-12-months-of-practice"},{"level":2,"text":"4. Level 4: Senior","anchor":"4-level-4-senior"}],"sizeBytes":6294,"modifiedAt":"2026-10-31T12:40:19.4973506+00:00"}